<description>
Since https://github.com/bitcoin-core/secp256k1/pull//1777 , libsecp256k1 allows an externally provided SHA256 compression function. This PR plug ours in, so it runs on our SHA-NI/ARMv8/SSE4 hardware optimized implementation instead of the libsecp bare internal one.

The biggest gains are at the signing side, not at the verification side.

Benchmarks at the bench introduction commit (43c9bdbf8ada1b581b5ab1f0a102517c243a60da) and at the tip:

On ARM64, `arm_shani` implementation

| benchmark | before | after | change |
|---|---|---|---|
| `ECDSASign` | 79.2 µs | 69.3 µs | -13% |
| `SchnorrSign` | 39.9 µs | 37.1 µs | -7% |
| `ECDSAVerify` | 27.4 µs | 27.3 µs | -0% |
| `SchnorrVerify` | 28.1 µs | 27.8 µs | -1% |
| `EllSwiftCreate` | 29.8 µs | 28.8 µs | -3% |
| `BIP324_ECDH` | 30.4 µs | 30.1 µs | -1% |
</description>

<commits>
9bd0886958 key: create the signing context on first use

Instead of requiring an ECC_Context to exist while signing, create the
secp256k1 signing context in a function-local static, the first time
GetSecp256k1SignContext() is called. It is never destroyed, so it stays
valid for threads still running at exit.

The RNG this depends on already initializes itself on first use, so no
explicit initialization order is needed. The blinding seed is obtained
through GetStrongRandBytes rather than GetRandBytes: the latter draws
from the deterministic RNG in tests, and consuming from it at whatever
point the context is first used would make test and fuzz results depend
on whether an earlier test or input happened to sign. Blinding does not
affect any outputs, so real randomness is harmless there. As a side
effect, BasicTestingSetup no longer consumes 32 bytes of the
deterministic RNG stream.

The context is never modified after creation, so return it as const.

ECC_Context is kept for now, merely creating the context eagerly. It is
removed in the next commit.

ce0e7a8c81 refactor: remove ECC_Context

It is no longer needed, as the signing context is created on first use.

The fuzz harness still creates the signing context up front, before the
coverage counters are reset, so that its one-time setup is not counted
as coverage of whichever input happens to sign first.

7a6ddc6160 bench: ECDSA and Schnorr signing and verification

Low-level signature creation and validation benchmarks.

df2e2b0223 libsecp: plug our hardware optimized SHA256 into libsecp256k1 ctx

libsecp256k1 lets a context use an external SHA256 compression
function. With ours, ECDSA signing gets ~15% faster on CPUs with
SHA extensions, Schnorr signing ~3%.

SHA256Transform dispatches to whichever implementation is selected at
the time of the call, so there is no need to run SHA256AutoDetect()
before creating the context. Doing so lazily would race with other
threads hashing, and override any explicitly selected implementation.

Also use the signing context instead of the static one for the BIP324
ECDH, which hashes inside libsecp256k1 too.

9a0d109501 libsecp: introduce public verification context

Verification uses secp256k1_context_static, which can't take our
optimized SHA256 compression function. Rather than reusing the signing
context, which blinds certain calculations, for no benefit to public
data, verification gets its own context. Like the signing context, it
is created on first use. As it needs no randomness, it lives next to
the verification code in pubkey.cpp, so users of the kernel library get
it without any setup.

Around 1% faster Schnorr signature verification. ECDSA remains equal
as hashing is done outside libsecp.

Co-authored-by: Pieter Wuille <bitcoin-dev@wuille.net>

c39f543c69 scripted-diff: use public verification context in pubkey.cpp

Every use of secp256k1_context_static in pubkey.cpp uses public
data, so all of them can use the verification context, which
contains our optimized SHA256 compression plugged in.

-BEGIN VERIFY SCRIPT-
sed -i -e '/\/\*/! s/secp256k1_context_static/GetSecp256k1PublicContext()/g' src/pubkey.cpp
-END VERIFY SCRIPT-

a90742ea56 test: check that signing and verification use the expected secp256k1 contexts

Temporarily install a call-counting SHA256 compression function in each
context, to check that ECDSA signing goes through the signing context
and Schnorr verification through the public one. Also check that both
contexts are distinct from each other and from the static one, and stay
the same across calls.

9c7f09ea7d use sign context for musig partial signing and bip352 scanning

secp256k1_musig_partial_sign() and secp256k1_silentpayments_recipient_scan_outputs()
take data that is not public, like the nonce or the scan key, so they should match
the rule operations on private data should use the signing context.

67ac80993d scripted-diff: use libsecp public context in musig and bip352

So all functions make use of our hardware optimized SHA256.

-BEGIN VERIFY SCRIPT-
sed -i -e '/\/\*/! s/secp256k1_context_static/GetSecp256k1PublicContext()/g' src/musig.cpp
sed -i -e '/\/\*/! s/secp256k1_context_static/GetSecp256k1PublicContext()/g' src/common/bip352.cpp
-END VERIFY SCRIPT-
